WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.860 --> 00:00:09.000
You might think this is Austria, or Switzerland but what if I told you this is Italy?

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.230
But… listen carefully.

00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:15.490
You’d expect for the people there to be
speaking Italian, right?

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:18.150
Well, in this case you’d be wrong.

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:19.640
The main spoken language here is…

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:20.640
GERMAN!

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:21.640
Of course, this is South Tyrol.

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:26.460
Yes, South Tyrol is in Italy, but they speak
German.

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:31.970
In fact, we think Italian is the main language
spoken in Italy but… nope.

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:36.520
Italy is full of dialects and languages that
are hardly understandable from one another.

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:40.070
Somebody from Veneto, in the North, could
hardly understand somebody from Sicily, in

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:41.070
the South.

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:42.070
Yes…

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:43.500
Italy is a country of contrasts…

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:46.800
And the same goes for their politics.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:53.200
Could you imagine a hard right party making
a coalition with a liberal populist one?

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:57.760
Could you imagine a Trump-like republican
making a coalition with someone who advocates

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:00.040
for a basic income for the poor?

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:03.320
Well… this is pretty much what they have
in Italy.

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:08.110
So how is that possible that right and left
can join forces to govern a country?

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:10.140
How is this possible?

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:19.280
In this video, we tell you the story…

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:20.470
Italy is a country of contradictions.

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:25.820
In one hand, it is the country where the Vatican
is: a country that maintains the respect for

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:26.820
their catholic traditions.

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:31.640
But on the other hand, they had Silvio Berlusconi,
a man famous for his orgies, as the prime

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:33.430
minister for years.

00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:40.670
On one hand, they are one of the most over
regulated countries from all across Europe,

00:01:40.670 --> 00:01:42.600
with red tape all over the place.

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:47.490
On the other hand, black economy here is bigger
than anywhere else.

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:52.580
If this doesn’t sound like enough contradiction
to you, then check this out: Italy’s government

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:57.220
is now a coalition of a far right wing party
and… a far left wing one.

00:01:57.220 --> 00:02:01.740
OK, is true, the 5 Star Movement is not exactly
a far left political party… at least, not

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:03.950
in the sense that Occasio Cortez would be.

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:08.310
But… how do you call a party that advocates
for things like a basic income for the poor

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:14.060
or… reducing the salary of the politicians
so they can look more like the working class?

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:20.360
Definitely, within the parliament, 5 stars
movement is as left as you can get in Italy,

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:21.709
excluding the communist that have little influence
in politics.

00:02:21.709 --> 00:02:22.770
And how about the far right wing?

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:24.210
The LEGA party is… also a contradiction
in itself.

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:28.990
Now they appear as an anti immigration, nationalist
party that claims the Italian first motto.

00:02:28.990 --> 00:02:31.560
But they were born as a secessionist movement!

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:36.180
In fact, originally, LEGA was cold LEGA NORD,
which means the Northern League.

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:39.230
And they wanted Italy to be splitted in two
different countries.

00:02:39.230 --> 00:02:44.250
Now… they have changed their minds and appear
as the paragon of Italian pride.

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:46.610
But hold on just a second… now you might
wonder…

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:48.250
Why do we care about this?

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:49.520
Well, first of all, its size.

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:58.640
Italy is the third biggest economy in the
Eurozone and they have the worst recession.

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:02.330
If they fall, Europe might fall too.

00:03:02.330 --> 00:03:05.790
And, if Europe falls, the rest of the world
might fall, too.

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:11.200
But also, the case of a coalition of opposite
extremes can help as a cautionary tale for

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:15.920
many other countries.

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:29.940
So yes, you want to know more about Italy.

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:34.980
So now the question is… how is that possible
that right and left could merge together?

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:39.440
Can this strange formula succeed in order
to bring the country out of the collapse?

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:43.400
Well, today we are going to answer to all
of this questions but, before we do, let’s

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:50.790
take a look back at the history.

00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:53.170
FROM LEGA NORD TO, JUST, LEGA

00:03:53.170 --> 00:03:59.459
As we said before, there are, approximately,
34 spoken languages and dialects in Italy.

00:03:59.459 --> 00:04:01.690
But the differences don’t stop here.

00:04:01.690 --> 00:04:08.640
You can also tell them… when it comes to
economics.

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:12.299
Southern Italy has a unemployment rate close
to 20%.

00:04:12.299 --> 00:04:16.949
In places like Sicily, water shortcuts were
a common thing until recently.

00:04:16.949 --> 00:04:19.019
And most of the people work on the agriculture…

00:04:19.019 --> 00:04:27.050
But, oh boy, Northern Italy is a totally different
country.

00:04:27.050 --> 00:04:30.490
This is the country were brands like Ferrari
or Lamborghini were born.

00:04:30.490 --> 00:04:35.229
Cities like Milano are worldwide famous for
their fashion industry, their factories and

00:04:35.229 --> 00:04:36.639
their high standards of living.

00:04:36.639 --> 00:04:40.300
Their unemployment rate is just a third of
that of the South.

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:42.901
And their income doubles that of the southerners.

00:04:42.901 --> 00:04:46.770
This big divide is what explains the birth
of the NORTHERN LEAGUE party in the 90s.

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:52.180
As I’ve said at the beginning of the video,
the party was founded as a regionalist secessionist

00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:53.180
movement.

00:04:53.180 --> 00:04:54.590
The forerunner was Umberto Bossi.

00:04:54.590 --> 00:04:59.580
Originally, he just wanted his own region,
Lombardy, where Milano is, to become independent

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:00.710
from Italy.

00:05:00.710 --> 00:05:04.520
But then , one day he thought “why not unify
several northern regionalist movements in

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:10.220
one?”

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:12.949
. This is how the biggest separatist movement
in Italy was born.

00:05:12.949 --> 00:05:16.089
Originally, it was not meant to be a right
or a left wing party.

00:05:16.089 --> 00:05:19.999
In fact, many of his founders came from anywhere
in the political spectrum.

00:05:19.999 --> 00:05:24.449
They had just one goal and one goal only:
split Italy in two halves.

00:05:24.449 --> 00:05:28.370
Basically, the country they want to create
was called PADANIA and it comprised all the

00:05:28.370 --> 00:05:30.119
Northern Italian regions.

00:05:30.119 --> 00:05:34.339
As any other separatist movement, they claimed
several cultural differences.

00:05:34.339 --> 00:05:38.800
But, unlike the Scottish separatism, the main
reason for this self determination movement

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:40.339
was… money!

00:05:40.339 --> 00:05:44.509
Basically, BOSSI claimed that Rome was stealing
the money from the richer regions to give

00:05:44.509 --> 00:05:45.509
it to the poor.

00:05:45.509 --> 00:05:48.009
So they would be better off going their own
way.

00:05:48.009 --> 00:05:53.490
And believe me, when it comes to hating Italy,
he was pretty straight ahead.

00:05:53.490 --> 00:05:58.309
“When I see the Italian flag, I feel like
shitting.

00:05:58.309 --> 00:06:02.929
I use the green, white and read to clean my
ass” –Umberto Bossi

00:06:02.929 --> 00:06:10.759
Bossi went as far as declaring Padania independent
from Italy in 1996.

00:06:10.759 --> 00:06:21.439
But, look, as separatists as they might be,
this Northern League was still an Italian

00:06:21.439 --> 00:06:22.439
party.

00:06:22.439 --> 00:06:25.770
This means they had to play by the Italian
rules and run for the Italian parliament.

00:06:25.770 --> 00:06:30.330
And, in order to expand their national influence,
they became one of the most loyal allies of

00:06:30.330 --> 00:06:31.330
Berlusconi.

00:06:31.330 --> 00:06:34.819
This helped them to get very good results
because their voters could see that, despite

00:06:34.819 --> 00:06:39.639
being a regional party, Northern League had
enough power to influence politics and bring

00:06:39.639 --> 00:06:43.490
lots of perks to the regions they represented.

00:06:43.490 --> 00:06:46.300
“Wake up, Padania!

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:48.919
With the Northern League, against the thieves
of Rome”

00:06:48.919 --> 00:06:53.139
But as influential as they could be, they
could only get as much as 8.3% of the votes.

00:06:53.139 --> 00:06:55.479
This means, it was still a small political
party…

00:06:55.479 --> 00:06:57.979
But thing changed in 2012.

00:06:57.979 --> 00:07:02.050
That was the year when Umberto Bossi suffered
a stroke and… he had to leave the party.

00:07:02.050 --> 00:07:08.713
His successor was this man you see on the
screen: MATEO SALVINI.

00:07:08.713 --> 00:07:11.719
And he had a radical change in mind for the
party.

00:07:11.719 --> 00:07:13.039
First of all, he changed the name.

00:07:13.039 --> 00:07:15.139
So it went from Northern League to just…

00:07:15.139 --> 00:07:16.139
League.

00:07:16.139 --> 00:07:21.169
And that kind of rebranding means one thing:
the party was evolving.

00:07:21.169 --> 00:07:25.690
The electoral program was not focused on the
dominance of the North over the South anymore.

00:07:25.690 --> 00:07:28.959
Finally, the enemy was not Rome anymore but…

00:07:28.959 --> 00:07:32.279
THE IMMIGRANTS!

00:07:32.279 --> 00:07:37.159
“More immigrants mean more criminals.”

00:07:37.159 --> 00:07:39.959
Salvini is working on a repatriation plan.

00:07:39.959 --> 00:07:41.940
And what happened to that anti Italian feeling?

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:45.159
Aren’t they the biggest enemy of Padanians
anymore?

00:07:45.159 --> 00:07:49.929
Well… if you think a party cannot change
its mind from one day to the other… think

00:07:49.929 --> 00:07:52.439
twice…

00:07:52.439 --> 00:07:56.209
‘Raise your head, Italy!

00:07:56.209 --> 00:07:58.309
I’m proud of being Italian.

00:07:58.309 --> 00:08:02.709
We are the best country in the World, we just
need a little bit of work and a little less

00:08:02.709 --> 00:08:04.550
taxes’ Matteo Salvini

00:08:04.550 --> 00:08:05.710
Boom!

00:08:05.710 --> 00:08:06.710
There you go!

00:08:06.710 --> 00:08:11.129
Now the former Northern League can apply to
all the Italian voters, both Southern and

00:08:11.129 --> 00:08:12.129
Northern.

00:08:12.129 --> 00:08:15.199
This means a bigger voting market and better
results.

00:08:15.199 --> 00:08:18.819
And I know what you might thing… can anybody
take seriously a party who can change their

00:08:18.819 --> 00:08:21.369
core ideology from one day to the other?

00:08:21.369 --> 00:08:24.059
The answer is…

00:08:24.059 --> 00:08:31.389
YES
Lega Victory at Italian elections, times are

00:08:31.389 --> 00:08:33.930
a-changing
But if this doesn’t seem crazy enought to

00:08:33.930 --> 00:08:39.180
you, let’s see the other player on this
game: the Italian populist left.

00:08:39.180 --> 00:08:45.779
Let’s have a look at it!

00:08:45.779 --> 00:08:46.910
THE BIG JOKE

00:08:46.910 --> 00:08:50.250
It’s the year 2009 and Italy is dealing
with a big crisis.

00:08:50.250 --> 00:08:54.759
The European Union wants budget cuts, and
Berlusconi and his government are in dire

00:08:54.759 --> 00:08:55.759
straits.

00:08:55.759 --> 00:08:58.670
That’s the fertile ground on which the FIVE
STARS MOVEMENT was founded.

00:08:58.670 --> 00:09:06.610
During those years a famous comedian, Beppe
Grillo, started a platform.

00:09:06.610 --> 00:09:10.940
He had little to no idea on how big his blog
and movement were about to become.

00:09:10.940 --> 00:09:15.880
Perhaps the most interesting moment of the
movement’s developing phase was the 2007

00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:18.010
Vaffanculo day in Bologna.

00:09:18.010 --> 00:09:20.810
Literally, that meant the ‘FUCK OFF day’.

00:09:20.810 --> 00:09:25.440
It was a huge event meant to collect signatures
to change the then electoral law.

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:29.440
They wanted to avoid people convicted of crimes
to run for parliamentary elections.

00:09:29.440 --> 00:09:35.440
But the real opportunity for Beppe Grillo
came some years later.

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:37.399
The Italian economy was still struggling.

00:09:37.399 --> 00:09:39.620
More and more citizens couldn’t make ends
meet.

00:09:39.620 --> 00:09:44.620
And this is when Italy gets a fully technocratic
president: Mario Monti.

00:09:44.620 --> 00:09:49.209
A technocrat is somebody who claims not to
have any ideology whatsoever: just tries to

00:09:49.209 --> 00:09:53.100
solve problems and manage politics on the
most effective way.

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:58.110
But to many Italians, this seemed to be an
imposition from the European Union, who wanted

00:09:58.110 --> 00:10:04.319
more and more budget cuts and more austerity.

00:10:04.319 --> 00:10:09.180
So this anger is what gave momentum to Beppe
Grillo and his Five Start Movement.

00:10:09.180 --> 00:10:13.220
The Movement started winning and placing its
members in regional and local cabinets.

00:10:13.220 --> 00:10:18.000
This momentum gave them the chance to run
in the 2013 general election where they were

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:23.579
the second most voted party after the Democratic
Party, defeating everyone’s expectations.

00:10:23.579 --> 00:10:26.160
They gained more than 8 million votes.

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:29.660
Which is a pretty huge number for a brand-new
party.

00:10:29.660 --> 00:10:35.930
The election’s results are in: huge victory
for the 5 Stars Movement.

00:10:35.930 --> 00:10:39.339
The web’s euphoria for the first party in
the Chamber of Deputies.

00:10:39.339 --> 00:10:43.830
The movement started as an anti-establishment
force and therefore at the beginning it didn’t

00:10:43.830 --> 00:10:46.550
seem to have a very detail-oriented plan.

00:10:46.550 --> 00:10:51.050
But all their work paid off and all those
voters gave them the push they needed.

00:10:51.050 --> 00:10:53.029
It was time for a detailed plan.

00:10:53.029 --> 00:10:54.850
It was time to become more structured.

00:10:54.850 --> 00:10:55.850
And then it came a big win: in 2016 Virginia
Raggi becomes Rome’s Mayor.

00:10:55.850 --> 00:10:56.850
The 5 Stars Movement conquers Rome: Raggi
elected mayor with 67% of the votes.

00:10:56.850 --> 00:10:57.850
“It’s an historic moment, the Roman citizens
have won”

00:10:57.850 --> 00:10:59.470
Their first huge win meant even more wind
under the movement’s wings.

00:10:59.470 --> 00:11:04.889
The movement’s ideology became clearer:
direct democracy as an evolution of representative

00:11:04.889 --> 00:11:05.889
democracy.

00:11:05.889 --> 00:11:09.329
The idea that the citizens will no longer
delegate their power to parties was at the

00:11:09.329 --> 00:11:12.509
base of Rousseau, an online voting platform.

00:11:12.509 --> 00:11:18.290
Through Rousseau the registered users of M5S
discuss, approve or reject legislative proposals.

00:11:18.290 --> 00:11:22.750
For example, the choice to support the abolition
of a law against immigrants was taken online

00:11:22.750 --> 00:11:30.069
by the movement’s members, even if the final
decision was against Grillo’s own opinion.

00:11:30.069 --> 00:11:37.079
Grillo, the M5S registered voters say no to
making illegal immigration a criminal offence.

00:11:37.079 --> 00:11:42.060
Another chapter of the party’s life began
when Luigi Di Maio came into power in 2017.

00:11:42.060 --> 00:11:46.709
And in 2018 he became Deputy Prime Minister
of Italy and Minister of Economic Development,

00:11:46.709 --> 00:11:48.459
Labour and Social Policies.

00:11:48.459 --> 00:11:53.589
The improbable ascent of the 31-year-old college
dropout and former soccer stadium usher is

00:11:53.589 --> 00:11:56.610
really a measure of the country’s turbulent
political landscape.

00:11:56.610 --> 00:12:01.839
And so, on one hand we have a seasoned right-wing
politician and on the other we have a 31-year-old

00:12:01.839 --> 00:12:02.839
freshman.

00:12:02.839 --> 00:12:10.199
And they were tied together in an unlikely
marriage by the 2018 election.

00:12:10.199 --> 00:12:12.889
OPEN MARRIAGE

00:12:12.889 --> 00:12:16.999
Italy is infamous for having weaker than usual
political alliances and parties.

00:12:16.999 --> 00:12:21.759
Since the country was proclaimed a republic
in 1946 it has had 29 Prime Ministers with

00:12:21.759 --> 00:12:24.840
an average government length of 2.5 years.

00:12:24.840 --> 00:12:27.450
Every election is a brand-new start for everyone.

00:12:27.450 --> 00:12:32.420
The last big change came in 2018, when a new
government was established after a very complicated

00:12:32.420 --> 00:12:33.720
post-election period.

00:12:33.720 --> 00:12:38.579
The new government is an alliance, as we said,
between the anti-establishment Five Star Movement

00:12:38.579 --> 00:12:40.249
and the far-right League.

00:12:40.249 --> 00:12:45.019
The populist wave split Italy in half: the
South voted for the 5 Star Movement and the

00:12:45.019 --> 00:12:53.709
North voted for the Lega, but both sides expressed
a vote of protest against more traditional

00:12:53.709 --> 00:12:55.420
parties like the Democratic Party.

00:12:55.420 --> 00:12:59.310
This new numbers show a real shift in Italy’s
electoral geography.

00:12:59.310 --> 00:13:04.259
The success of the two most anti-establishment
forces is mostly due to economic uncertainty.

00:13:04.259 --> 00:13:06.759
The outsiders have become the mainstream.

00:13:06.759 --> 00:13:10.480
Immediately after the elections, the 5 Star
Movement, the party that got the most votes

00:13:10.480 --> 00:13:12.500
needed to find a coalition party.

00:13:12.500 --> 00:13:18.009
The choice was the Lega.

00:13:18.009 --> 00:13:22.300
Italy election: Populist Five Star and League
vie for power

00:13:22.300 --> 00:13:26.139
But this marriage wasn’t an easy one, it
took 10 weeks to reach an agreement.

00:13:26.139 --> 00:13:28.939
The two parties were adversaries before the
election.

00:13:28.939 --> 00:13:33.379
But at the same time, they became the largest
groups able to muster a majority in both houses.

00:13:33.379 --> 00:13:40.870
And so, the 5 Star brought to the table their
32% and the League its 17% and the new government

00:13:40.870 --> 00:13:41.980
began.

00:13:41.980 --> 00:13:47.810
We’re now a few months in and the two deputy
Prime Ministers, Salvini and Di Maio are doing

00:13:47.810 --> 00:13:51.140
the most to fulfill the promises they made
to their voters.

00:13:51.140 --> 00:13:56.430
Salvini is trying to crack down on immigration
and to pass a flat tax rate of 15% for companies

00:13:56.430 --> 00:14:00.939
and individuals, which experts say it would
cut tax revenues by 80 billion Euros per Year.

00:14:00.939 --> 00:14:04.350
Di Maio is trying to make a huge basic income
reform happen.

00:14:04.350 --> 00:14:09.019
It’s a universal income for the poor that
would cost an estimated 17 billion Euros per

00:14:09.019 --> 00:14:10.019
year.

00:14:10.019 --> 00:14:13.480
The two leaders are basically in an open marriage:
they’re together but they’re still looking

00:14:13.480 --> 00:14:14.990
around for partners.

00:14:14.990 --> 00:14:18.120
Both parties are staying together, but they
are still trying to get the biggest political

00:14:18.120 --> 00:14:20.800
capital possible for the next election cycle.

00:14:20.800 --> 00:14:25.670
But pleasing an electorate is in itself a
very difficult feat to accomplish, even without

00:14:25.670 --> 00:14:27.430
further obstacles.

00:14:27.430 --> 00:14:34.569
La dolce vita slips away again as Italy tumbles
back into recession

00:14:34.569 --> 00:14:38.749
The country is now in a recession, just as
the Italian Deputy Prime Ministers stated

00:14:38.749 --> 00:14:42.100
that Italy was on the cusp of an economic
miracle.

00:14:42.100 --> 00:14:45.059
In 2018 the GDP decreased 0.2%.

00:14:45.059 --> 00:14:50.620
This is the third recession in 10 years, but
the Italian PM says not to worry.

00:14:50.620 --> 00:14:55.350
He stated that a recession is “determined
by the European economic cycle” whatever

00:14:55.350 --> 00:14:56.380
that means…

00:14:56.380 --> 00:14:59.620
While politicians are playing the “blame
it on the people who were here before” card,

00:14:59.620 --> 00:15:03.470
many Italians are extremely worried on how
this recession could have an impact on their

00:15:03.470 --> 00:15:05.309
lives for years to come.

00:15:05.309 --> 00:15:10.899
Italy has the second largest debt after Greece,
but its economy is eight times larger and

00:15:10.899 --> 00:15:14.800
a crisis this big could severely cripple the
Eurozone.

00:15:14.800 --> 00:15:18.850
So now the question is, is it possible to
see a similar alliance in other countries?

